A clustered index doesn't just point to your data; it is your data. 
It dictates the actual physical sorting order of the rows on the disk.
Because a table's rows can only be physically sorted in one sequential order, you can only have exactly one clustered index per table.

Technical Breakdown :-
Non-Clustered Index: Think of this like the index at the back of a textbook.
The index is sorted alphabetically, but it only contains pointers (page numbers) to where the actual information lives.
Searching requires two steps: reading the index, then jumping to the specific data page.

Clustered Index: Think of this like a dictionary. 
The data itself is physically stored in alphabetical order.
In database terms, the clustered index forms a B-Tree where the 'leaf nodes' contain the actual data rows themselves, not just pointers.

Note :- When you define a Primary Key in most relational databases (like PostgreSQL or SQL Server), the engine automatically creates a clustered index on that key by default.

Business Context :-

Clustered indexes are wildly powerful for range queries.
If a business analyst frequently queries a sales table for date BETWEEN '2023-01-01' AND '2023-01-31', and the table has a clustered index on the date column,
the disk head finds the start date and simply reads sequentially. There is no jumping around the disk, making the I/O lightning fast.

Design Decision :- 
In modern cloud data warehouses like Snowflake or BigQuery, traditional B-Tree clustered indexes don't exist.
Instead, we use concepts like 'Clustering Keys' or 'Partitioning'. While the underlying mechanism shifts to columnar micro-partitions,
the data engineering principle remains identical: we intentionally physically co-locate data based on how downstream analysts filter it (usually by time or tenant ID) 
to maximize 'partition pruning' and minimize compute costs.
